# C. AUTHORITY BOUNDARIES AND ECOSYSTEM

## PM-AUTHORITY-001 — Prime/Core Ownership

Prime OS/Core owns or mediates:

- Host identity and lineage;
- Security-State continuity;
- Core authority;
- Auth/Login-Session;
- Provider admission/trust;
- capability registry;
- Application Profiles;
- Workload Policies;
- live workload registry;
- process/service authority;
- hardware graph;
- resource accounting/enforcement;
- dependency/control-session truth;
- assessments;
- AuthorizationDecision;
- Host Actions;
- Security Audit;
- Host Action Evidence;
- quiescence/transitions;
- disclosure;
- support applicability.

## PM-AUTHORITY-002 — Manager Ownership

Manager owns presentation, navigation, search/filter/sort, visualization, bounded presentation history, non-authoritative preferences/cache, selection/follow state, request UX, evidence UX, and diagnostic UX.

## PM-AUTHORITY-003 — No Direct Privileged Bypass

Manager never obtains Host authority merely by:

- UID 0;
- broad privileged socket groups;
- generic root shell;
- direct `/proc`/`/sys` mutation;
- direct cgroup/systemd authority.

## PM-ECO-001 — AgentOps Boundary

AgentOps owns semantic durable Operations. Manager interactive fleet fan-out is not a hidden AgentOps replacement.

## PM-ECO-002 — Ptah Boundary

Ptah may impose stronger higher-level constraints. It does not silently weaken Prime safety.

## PM-ECO-003 — Origins Boundary

Host discovery/aggregation is not Host-control authority.

## PM-ECO-004 — Hunter / Oracle / Grid-Knight Boundary

These are separate consumers/advisory systems with their own authorization/disclosure scopes. Their conclusions remain derived/advisory and do not automatically become Prime machine truth, protection, risk, or authorization.

## PM-ECO-005 — Terminal Boundary

Prime Terminal owns PTY/session/command semantics. Manager is not another Terminal.

## PM-ECO-006 — Recovery Boundary

Prime Recovery remains independent emergency/break-glass authority. Manager never silently becomes Recovery.

