# D. TRUST MODEL AND MANAGER PROCESS ARCHITECTURE

## PM-TCB-001 — Trusted Computing Base

For a privileged local operation, the TCB includes as applicable:

- kernel / Prime security primitives;
- current Core authority;
- Auth/Login-Session authority;
- trusted interaction path;
- minimal Manager broker;
- operation-scoped authoritative Provider;
- release/support trust root where production eligibility is safety-bearing.

## PM-TCB-002 — Untrusted UI Domain

Treat as untrusted:

- Manager renderer;
- WebView renderer/GPU/network/utility subprocesses;
- arbitrary executable metadata;
- Provider-supplied text/artwork/content;
- external web content.

## PM-TCB-003 — Out-of-Scope TCB Failure

Manager cannot guarantee security if the kernel, current Core, Auth authority, trusted interaction authority, support trust root, or owner-controlled Recovery authority is compromised.

## PM-ARCH-001 — Renderer/Broker Split

```text
untrusted UI process tree
        ↓
typed origin-bound bridge
        ↓
minimal trusted broker
        ↓
authenticated current-Core IPC
```

## PM-ARCH-002 — Renderer Restrictions

Renderer and UI subprocesses cannot:

- open Core IPC;
- inherit Core handles;
- receive reusable credentials/grants;
- retain authoritative preflight/fence material;
- ptrace/debug/inject broker;
- access broker memory;
- execute Provider HTML/JS in bridge-enabled context;
- intentionally persist protected Host data through browser storage.

